          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Unnamed first-person narrator\",\"addressee\":\"Maria\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig. Jeg elsker Dig i Lyst og i\\nNød, Hverdage og Festdage. Du er den, jeg vil dø med, men først vil\\njeg leve meget, meget længe med Dig. Javel har jeg længtes efter\\nmin yndige Elskerindes Kærtegn. Men mest har jeg længtes efter alt\\ndet andet, som ogsaa er Dig: dit trofaste Hjærte, din rene Tanke,\\ndin milde Tale, dit lyse Smil, som er min Sol i Tvivlen, dit gode\\nHaandtryk, som er min Styrke paa Vejen.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal words directly avow the speaker's present love as true. The oath-like construction presents the avowal with emphatic certainty, directly answering Maria's concern that his apparent change might be temporary or imagined.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"elsker jeg Dig\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig.\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":3,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"In this occurrence, the avowal also substantially affirms an enduring relational commitment. Maria has asked whether it is something he will regret tomorrow, and the same turn defines his love as holding through pleasure and distress, ordinary and festive days, and as oriented toward a long shared life. These clauses are not merely an unrelated nearby promise: they specify the durable force with which he says he loves her. Nevertheless, the focal phrase's lexical core remains an avowal, and the precise practical terms of the undertaking are not specified.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"For det er jo sandt, ikke noget, Du imorgen fortryder, ikke\\nnoget, Du nu bilder Dig ind, fordi Du har længtes en Smule efter din\\nElskerinde?\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg elsker Dig i Lyst og i\\nNød, Hverdage og Festdage. Du er den, jeg vil dø med, men først vil\\njeg leve meget, meget længe med Dig.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"Although the reunion is highly emotional, the focal expression is a deliberate and articulated answer to Maria's question, followed by reasons and clarification. It is not represented as a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Jeg svarer: »Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Men mest har jeg længtes efter alt\\ndet andet, som ogsaa er Dig: dit trofaste Hjærte, din rene Tanke,\\ndin milde Tale, dit lyse Smil, som er min Sol i Tvivlen, dit gode\\nHaandtryk, som er min Styrke paa Vejen.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.9,\"reason\":\"The expression also reassures Maria, but that function is adequately explained by its emphatic truth-directed avowal and its contextually established affirmation of enduring commitment. No separate core force remains unsupported by T and P.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"For det er jo sandt, ikke noget, Du imorgen fortryder, ikke\\nnoget, Du nu bilder Dig ind\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Saavist som jeg altid hellere har sagt Dig for lidt\\nend for meget, saavist elsker jeg Dig.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"At their reunion, Maria asks the narrator to confirm that his changed attitude is genuine, not a passing effect of longing and not something he will regret the next day. He answers her directly with the focal avowal and immediately characterizes his love as enduring across hardship, ordinary life, celebration, and a long shared future.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The narrator affirms that he is bound to a durable loving life with Maria—through pleasure and distress and through everyday and festive life—and voices an intention to live with her for a long time. Maria's tears and happiness are represented as understanding and accepting the truth of what he says: “hun forstaar, jeg taler sandt, og fordi hun er lykkelig.” The scene does not specify formal terms such as marriage, so the undertaking is relational and enduring rather than contractually precise.\",\"recognised_work\":false,\"recognition_note\":\"I do not recognize the work from the supplied evidence.\",\"limitations\":\"The supplied scene strongly represents sincerity and uptake, but it cannot independently establish the speaker's inner state beyond the literary narration. It also leaves the exact institutional or practical terms of the shared-life commitment unspecified.\"}"
